// Runtime/ManagedDirectoryRuntime.maxon — the `__ManagedDirectory` box: one Win32 SEARCH, closed on drop (R4.3).
//
// ⭐ **THE ORACLE IS `specs/managed-directory.md`, AND IT IS THE ONLY ORACLE AVAILABLE**, exactly as
// `ManagedFileRuntime`'s header records for the file family: `run_program compiler=csharp` crashes (`E9001`)
// on a standalone `__Managed*` call, so the ten committed cases are the whole of what can be asked.
//
// ⭐ **HOST-ONLY BY THE SAME RULING (2026-07-30).** `x64-windows` is real; every other target refuses at the
// call site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct` via `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, which
// matches on the `__md_` PREFIX — so a method added later is gated by construction rather than by memory.
//
// ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RECORD IS TWO-LEVEL, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THE TWO REFERENCES DISAGREE WITH EACH OTHER.**
// A `__ManagedDirectory` is an 8-byte BOX whose single `_block` field points at a separately allocated FIND
// BLOCK holding the search handle, the 320-byte `WIN32_FIND_DATAA` the OS refills, and the advance-first
// pending flag. Both type-checkers DECLARE that shape (8 bytes, one field: bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:1449-1463`,
// v1 `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1903-1919`) and only the bootstrap's runtime AGREES with it
// (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:4104-4111`: `mm_alloc(8)` whose `_block` points at the 336-byte block built at
// `:2349-2364`). v1's runtime hands back the bare 40-byte block AS the handle
// (`runtime.std:2782-2787, 2840-2846`), so v1's own 8-byte declaration is a fiction, and on Windows it grows a
// THIRD shape (a 616-byte inner wrapper, `X64Backend.maxon:12679`).
// ⇒ The bootstrap is self-consistent and v1 is not, and the 8-byte box is already shv2's shape for this
// family (`ManagedFileRuntime.ManagedFileBoxBytes`), so the front end's `StructLayout` and the runtime's
// allocation are held together by the same drift guard (`SignatureIndex.managedDirectoryLayout`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY A SEPARATE BLOCK AT ALL, RATHER THAN A 336-BYTE BOX?** Because the box is what user code HOLDS —
// it is stored into a `var dir as __ManagedDirectory` field, copied between owners, and refcounted — while
// the find record is what the OS WRITES THROUGH for the whole life of the search. Fusing them would put a
// kernel-written 320-byte buffer inside a refcounted user aggregate, and `close()` would then have no way to
// release the OS resource without releasing the memory the still-live box is made of. The split is what lets
// `close()` be idempotent and independent of the drop, which is the contract the corpus depends on.
//
// ⚠ **DO NOT PORT v1's arm64 DIRECTORY WALK.** `Arm64Backend.maxon:2403-2411` has no macOS import wired,
// returns a raw `-1`, and `mrt_directory_next`'s `<= 0` end test absorbs that as END OF LISTING — an empty
// result with no error. It is v1's macOS lane only; this rung is the x64-windows path, which does not share it.

// ⭐ **THE BOX — its field offset and its size**, asserted against the registered `StructLayout`
// (`SignatureIndex.managedDirectoryLayout`) rather than agreeing with it by inspection, exactly as
// `ManagedFileHandleOffset`/`ManagedFileBoxBytes` are: the SHAPE is stated for the front end by that layout
// and written here by these constants, and two readers of one shape is the pair this project keeps finding
// drifted.
//
// One 8-byte slot holding the find block's address. `specs/managed-directory.md`'s "Type Structure" names the
// single `_block` field, and the corpus never reads it — direct construction is refused (E3072) so the
// pointer cannot be forged, and every read is the runtime's own through this offset.
export let ManagedDirectoryBlockOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedDirectoryBoxBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIND BLOCK — the bootstrap's layout, transcribed** (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:2349-2364`):
//
//   [0  .. 7]    the `FindFirstFileA` search HANDLE
//   [8  .. 327]  the 320-byte `WIN32_FIND_DATAA` the OS refills on every `FindNextFileA`
//   [328..335]   the advance-first PENDING flag
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE PENDING FLAG IS NOT BOOKKEEPING — WITHOUT IT THE FIRST ENTRY OF EVERY SEARCH IS SILENTLY LOST.**
// `FindFirstFileA` does not merely open a search, it RETURNS THE FIRST ENTRY, already written into the
// record. `next()` is documented advance-first ("advances the cursor to the next REAL entry"), so a naive
// `next()` that calls `FindNextFileA` immediately skips over the entry the OPEN already fetched. The flag
// says "the record already holds an unconsumed entry": the first `next()` consumes it in place and only
// later ones fetch. Both references do exactly this, and the bootstrap's own comment says "Do NOT
// pre-consume here."
// ⚠ It is invisible to the corpus's search patterns by luck, not by design: a `dir/*` pattern's first two
// entries are `.` and `..`, which the dot filter discards anyway — so a missing flag would pass
// `search-and-list` and lose the first REAL entry of any pattern that does not match them (`dir/*.txt`).
// `managed-directory.next-does-not-skip-the-first-match` is the case that would catch it.
//
// ⚠ The flag is a full WORD rather than the bootstrap's byte. The Std tier's `accessWidthFor` carries `byte`
// and `word64` and nothing between, so either would lower; a word costs the same 336 bytes (328 + 8 is the
// bootstrap's own total) and keeps every access in this file at one width.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE TWO TAIL NUMBERS ARE DERIVED, NOT TRANSCRIBED, AND THAT IS THE POINT** (review, R4.3). `328` and
// `336` are `8 + FindDataBytes` and that plus one word — so writing them as literals would be a THIRD copy
// of "a `WIN32_FIND_DATAA` is 320 bytes", the first two being `ManagedFileRuntime.FindDataBytes` (which
// `stat`/`exists` allocate by) and the Win32 header itself. Nothing would make the copies agree: correct
// the size in one place and this block silently UNDER-ALLOCATES, at which point the kernel writes the tail
// of the find record straight over the pending flag on every `FindNextFileA` — a wrong answer with no
// compile error and no failing test. `FindDataBytes` is exported for exactly this reader.
let FindBlockHandleOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let FindBlockFindDataOffset = 8 as ByteOffset

// The pending flag's own width — see above for why it is a word and not the bootstrap's byte.
let FindBlockPendingBytes = 8 as ParsedInt
let FindBlockPendingOffset = ((FindBlockFindDataOffset as ParsedInt) + FindDataBytes) as ByteOffset
let FindBlockBytes = (FindBlockPendingOffset as ParsedInt) + FindBlockPendingBytes

// The pending flag's two values. Named rather than spelled `1`/`0` at four sites: the flag reads as a
// direction ("consume in place" vs "fetch") and a bare literal at a branch says neither.
let FindEntryPending = 1 as ParsedInt
let FindEntryConsumed = 0 as ParsedInt

// The handle value a CLOSED block carries — `ManagedFileClosedHandle`'s twin, and zero for its reason: the
// find block is allocated zeroed, so a block whose `FindFirstFileA` never ran and one that has been closed
// carry the same value and ONE guard covers both. A search handle the OS actually returns is never 0
// (`FindFirstFileA` reports failure as `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`, which is -1).
let ManagedDirectoryClosedHandle = 0 as ParsedInt

// `next()`'s two answers, per `specs/managed-directory.md`: "returns non-zero if one was found or 0 when the
// iteration is complete". EOF is a plain 0 RETURN and never a throw — both references agree — so only a real
// OS error reaches `nextFailed`.
let DirectoryEntryFound = 1 as ParsedInt
let DirectoryIterationComplete = 0 as ParsedInt

// ASCII `.` — the leading byte of the `.` / `..` pseudo-entries. **The dot filtering lives in the RUNTIME,
// not the stdlib**: both references filter inside `next` (bootstrap `:2433-2451`, v1 `runtime.std:3093-3122`),
// and the spec states it as the method's contract ("The runtime owns all dot-filtering, so `filename()` never
// returns `.` or `..`").
let DotByte = 0x2E as ParsedInt

// The NUL that ends a cstring — what ends `cFileName`, what tells `.` from `.x` and `..` from `..x`, what
// `emitCStringLength` walks to, and what `CommandLineRuntime` terminates each scanned argument with.
// EXPORTED once that fourth reader arrived rather than re-spelled `0` beside it: a `0` at a byte compare
// says nothing about whether it means "end of string" or "the number zero".
export let NulByte = 0 as ParsedInt

// `ERROR_NO_MORE_FILES` — the ONE `GetLastError` reading that makes a FALSE from `FindNextFileA` an ordinary
// end of iteration rather than a failure. Everything else is a real error and throws `nextFailed`.
let ErrorNoMoreFiles = 18 as ParsedInt

// `INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES` — what `GetFileAttributesA` answers for a path that names nothing. It is the
// full DWORD, so it is compared against a DWORD-masked load rather than against -1.
let InvalidFileAttributes = 0xFFFFFFFF as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **`MAX_PATH` — THE FIRST BUFFER EVERY Win32 PATH FILL IN THIS COMPILER ASKS FOR.** `GetCurrentDirectoryA(n, buf)`
// answers three different things: `0` is failure, `< n` is "written, NUL included", and `>= n` is "too
// small — and this is the size it needs, NUL included", in which case the buffer's contents are UNDEFINED.
// So the first attempt uses `MAX_PATH` (which the bootstrap also starts at,
// `X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:4102`) and a second attempt uses exactly what the OS asked for. A THIRD attempt
// cannot be needed: the second buffer is the size the OS itself named.
//
// ⚠ EXPORTED for `ProcessRuntime` (W5 review), whose `GetModuleFileNameA` walk starts at the same 260 for
// the same reason. It briefly held its own `FirstPathAttemptBytes = 260`, which is ONE Win32 constant
// written down twice: the day a measurement says a bigger first attempt is right, one of the two would move
// and nothing would make the other follow. The two loops differ in their RETRY POLICY, not in what
// `MAX_PATH` is.
export let MaxPathBytes = 260 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedDirectoryError` ORDINALS, AS ERROR FLAGS (`ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`)** — the
// `ManagedFileRuntime` discipline, and the ordinals are positional facts about the case order of the enum
// DECLARED at `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:190` (A1s). `verifyManagedDirectoryOrdinals` below is what holds these
// literals to that declaration.
//
// ⚠ **THE SOURCE OF THESE NUMBERS IS `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:189-199`, NOT A COMMENT.** The bootstrap's own
// comment at `2-Parser.cs:1442-1444` lists SEVEN cases and omits `iteratorInvalid`, which shifts every
// ordinal after it by one; the enum three lines below that comment has eight. It is the THIRD reference
// comment found stale about its own enum, after `__ManagedMemoryError`'s ordinals and `osFileOpenRead`'s
// `CreateFileW`.
//
// ⚠ `iteratorInvalid` (4) has no flag here because nothing throws it: it names "filename() on an exhausted
// iterator", and `filename()` is non-throwing in both references and in the spec. The case is DECLARED (the
// ordinals after it depend on its slot) and deliberately unreachable — which is why its ORDINAL is here even
// though its flag is not, and why `claimManagedDirectoryOrdinals` states it as `neverThrown` rather than
// leaving a gap a corpus reorder could move in silence (W135).
let ManagedDirectoryNotFoundOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedOrdinal = 1 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedOrdinal = 2 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryNextFailedOrdinal = 3 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryIteratorInvalidOrdinal = 4 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedOrdinal = 5 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedOrdinal = 6 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryClosedOrdinal = 7 as ParsedInt
let ManagedDirectoryNotFoundFlag = ManagedDirectoryNotFoundOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedFlag = ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedFlag = ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedDirectoryNextFailedFlag = ManagedDirectoryNextFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedFlag = ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedFlag = ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedDirectoryClosedFlag = ManagedDirectoryClosedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// The `__md_` prefix that identifies EVERY directory runtime entry point — the one test `scanRuntimeUsage`
// reads to set `usesManagedDirectory`, and the one `SemanticCheck` reads for E3104.
let ManagedDirectoryRuntimePrefix = b"__md_"

// ⭐ **THE EIGHT ENTRY POINTS.** Seven name a `__ManagedDirectory` method — the surface BOTH references
// declare, line for line — and `__md_destruct` is the compiler-INSERTED one: the drop a box takes when its
// last owner goes away (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`), which is the whole of the RAII half.
export let ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchName = b"__md_open_search"
export let ManagedDirectoryNextName = b"__md_next"
export let ManagedDirectoryFilenameName = b"__md_filename"
export let ManagedDirectoryCloseName = b"__md_close"
export let ManagedDirectoryDestructName = b"__md_destruct"
export let ManagedDirectoryExistsName = b"__md_exists"
export let ManagedDirectoryCreateName = b"__md_create"
export let ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathName = b"__md_current_path"

// ⭐ **WHICH `__ManagedDirectory` CALLEES THROW** — the fourth composer behind `Parser.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`.
// Held to the builders by the bidirectional drift guard in `installManagedDirectoryRuntime`, copied from
// `installManagedFileRuntime` for its stated reason: a predicate and a `throws` stamp are one fact in two
// places, and this project keeps finding that pair drifted.
export function isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	// ⚠ **THE FOUR ABSENTEES ARE EACH ABSENT FOR A STATED REASON, not by oversight.**
	//   • `__md_close` — the spec documents `close()` as idempotent and destructor-called; naming it here
	//     would make a `try` REQUIRED on a call that cannot fail (E3055).
	//   • `__md_destruct` — a DROP has no caller to hand an error to; it runs at a scope exit nobody wrote.
	//   • `__md_exists` — `false` is a valid answer ("it is not a directory"), not a failure. Both references
	//     register it non-throwing for that reason.
	//   • `__md_filename` — the spec's "Instance Methods (non-throwing)". It reads a record the OPEN already
	//     filled, so there is no call to fail.
	return callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchName) or callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryNextName) or callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryCreateName) or callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathName)
end 'isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE USER ACTUALLY WROTE, FOR THE DIAGNOSTICS THAT QUOTE A CALLEE BACK AT THEM.**
// `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall`'s E3057 names the call that needed a `try`, and `specs/managed-directory.md`
// pins that sentence: `throwing function requires try: 'next'`. The user never wrote `__md_next`, so the
// runtime name would be a symbol they have never heard of — the same objection `TargetUnsupportedUnnamedSubject`
// records for E3104.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A MAP ONTO THE PARSER'S OWN CONSTANTS, NOT A SECOND SPELLING.** Each arm answers with the very
// `ManagedDirectory*Method` byte string `parseManagedDirectoryStaticCall`/`dispatchManagedDirectoryMethod`
// dispatched on, so there is nothing here that can disagree with what the source is matched against.
// It is asked only on the COLD path, and only for the four THROWING callees — the other four cannot reach
// either diagnostic at all, which is what the panic states rather than answers.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AS OF D11c IT HAS A SECOND READER, AND THE `create` ARM IS ITS ALONE (D11c review).** `Parser`'s E3059
// asks this same map for a VALUELESS `try`, and `__md_create` is the family's one void throwing member — so
// that arm has no E3057 case and E3059 is the only thing that can observe it. `specs-shv2/void-call-result.md`
// pins it there rather than here, beside the other eight callees E3059 can reach, because the roster is
// "throwing AND void" and that crosses every family. **MEASURED: with this arm answering `next`, the whole
// suite read 2897/0 while an author who wrote `create` was told about `'next'`.**
export function managedDirectorySourceMethodName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchName) 'openSearch'
		return ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchMethod
	end 'openSearch'
	if callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryNextName) 'next'
		return ManagedDirectoryNextMethod
	end 'next'
	if callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryCreateName) 'create'
		return ManagedDirectoryCreateMethod
	end 'create'
	if callee.equals(ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathName) 'currentPath'
		return ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathMethod
	end 'currentPath'
	panic("managedDirectorySourceMethodName: no source-method arm for '{String.from(callee)}'. EITHER it is not a throwing `__ManagedDirectory` callee — this map is asked ONLY behind `isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee`, so nothing else can reach an E3057 — OR that predicate gained a name and this map did not, in which case add an arm answering the parser's own `ManagedDirectory*Method` constant. `installManagedDirectoryRuntime`'s drift guard asks this for every throwing `__md_` builder it installs, so the second case fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`")
end 'managedDirectorySourceMethodName'

// ⭐ **THE ERROR TYPE A DIRECTORY CALLEE THROWS — the WHAT to the predicate's THAT**, and the twin of
// `managedFileThrows`. `ProgramSignatures.throwsOf` answers with it for a callee no file declares, which is
// what types an `otherwise (e)` binding and therefore what makes `match e` see an enum instead of a scalar;
// the four throwing builders stamp it as their `throws` clause. ONE speller for one fact.
export function managedDirectoryThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(ManagedDirectoryErrorTypeName))
end 'managedDirectoryThrows'

// The three-flag errno classification (`ManagedFileRuntime.emitErrnoClassifiedThrow`) with THIS family's two
// fixed cases supplied. `__ManagedDirectoryError`'s `notFound`/`accessDenied` sit at the same two ordinals
// `__ManagedFileError`'s do — both references order both enums that way — but they are named here rather than
// inherited, so that coincidence is a fact this file states about its own enum instead of a silent
// dependency on another one's. Only the catch-all differs per operation.
function emitDirectoryErrnoClassifiedThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, err ValueId, fallbackFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	emitErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: block, err: err, notFoundFlag: ManagedDirectoryNotFoundFlag, deniedFlag: ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedFlag, fallbackFlag: fallbackFlag, ids: ids)
end 'emitDirectoryErrnoClassifiedThrow'

// The find block a live box points at. Every instance entry point starts here.
function emitLoadFindBlock(module StdModule, block IrBlock, box ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: box, offset: ManagedDirectoryBlockOffset, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadFindBlock'

// The address of the find block's `WIN32_FIND_DATAA`, which is what every Win32 find call is handed.
function emitFindDataAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, findBlock ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let offset = emitConst(module, block: block, value: FindBlockFindDataOffset as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: findBlock, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitFindDataAddr'

// The address of `cFileName` inside the find block — the record's offset plus the field's, so the two
// numbers are added in ONE place rather than pre-summed into a third constant that could disagree with them.
function emitFileNameAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, findBlock ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let offset = emitConst(module, block: block, value: (FindBlockFindDataOffset + FindDataFileNameOffset) as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: findBlock, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitFileNameAddr'

// ⭐ **strlen — WALK A NUL-TERMINATED cstring AND LEAVE ITS LENGTH IN THE CONTINUATION BLOCK.**
//
// ⚠ **EXTRACTED FROM `emitCStringToManaged` WHEN THE COMMAND-LINE RUNTIME BECAME ITS SECOND ASKER.**
// `__cl_count`/`__cl_arg` size their scratch buffer by the command line's length and do not want the copy,
// so the measure and the build are now two builders rather than one — which is also what makes the length
// available to a caller that is not about to allocate an `Array` record.
//
// `length`'s `ValueId` is MINTED BY THE CALLER and used as the walk's loop-carried cursor. At the moment the
// header exits, that cursor IS the length, and it reaches the continuation block with NO block-arg of its
// own: the header dominates the continuation (its `condBranch` is the only way in), so the value is simply
// in scope — the same dominance argument `ManagedFileRuntime.emitPathToCString` makes for its own `len`.
export function emitCStringLength(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cstr ValueId, length ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdslh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdslb")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdsld")

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(length, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let ch = emitLoadByte(module, block: header, base: cstr, index: length, ids: ids)
	let nul = emitConst(module, block: header, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: ch, b: nul, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let next = emitBin(module, block: body, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(next)))

	return done
end 'emitCStringLength'

// One byte of a NUL-terminated cstring, zero-extended.
//
// ⚠ EXPORTED because `CommandLineRuntime`'s splitter reads the command line a byte at a time and this is
// exactly that read. A second copy would be a second answer to "how wide is a cstring byte, and is it
// zero-extended?" — the pair this project keeps finding drifted.
export function emitLoadByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let addr = emitBin(module, block: block, a: base, b: index, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let loaded = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(loaded, addrId: addr, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, valueType: StdType.u8))
	return loaded
end 'emitLoadByte'

// One byte INTO a raw buffer — `emitLoadByte`'s exact dual, and the one writer of a `u8` at this tier.
//
// ⚠ **IT LIVES BESIDE ITS DUAL RATHER THAN BESIDE ITS FIRST CALLER (W5).** It was file-private in
// `CommandLineRuntime`, which made the pair "how wide is a raw byte, and is it zero-extended?" answerable
// from two files with only one of the two answers exported — so the second family that needed to WRITE a
// byte (`__proc_exe_path`, NUL-terminating an unavailable path) would have had to copy it. The load and the
// store are one fact about one representation; they are now one paragraph.
export function emitStoreByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, index ValueId, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let addr = emitBin(module, block: block, a: base, b: index, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(addr, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, valueId: value, valueType: StdType.u8))
end 'emitStoreByte'

// ⭐⭐ **A NUL-TERMINATED cstring → A FRESH OWNED `__ManagedMemory`, AND THE CALLER OWNS THE RESULT.**
// `filename()` and `currentPath()` both answer with one, and both would otherwise be twenty lines of the
// same loop.
//
// ⭐ **THIS IS v1's SHAPE (`runtime.std:3273-3290`), NOT THE BOOTSTRAP's, AND THE DIVERGENCE IS THE POINT.**
// The bootstrap's `filename()` returns a RAW POINTER into the find block (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:2374-2386`
// hands back `block + 52`) while its type-checker declares the result `__ManagedMemory` — so the value is a
// cstring wearing a record's type, it is invalidated by the very next `next()`, and `close()` leaves it
// dangling. v1 allocates and copies, and shv2 must: a `__ManagedMemory` here is a real `Array` record since
// R4.4 and `String.init(dir.filename())` reads its `length@8`/`buffer@0`, which a cstring pointer does not
// have. Copying is both the safe answer and the only one that types.
//
// The record and its buffer come from `ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitManagedByteBuffer`, which is where the
// `__managed_create` + `__managed_reserve` pair and the reason for it now live — `__con_read_stdin` (W5) fills the
// same kind of record from an OS read instead of from a copy, and the two must not grow two answers to
// "how is a byte-element `Array` record made?".
//
// Threads through `cur` and returns the continuation block: the strlen loop and the copy loop each add
// blocks, so the caller carries on from the one this leaves it in. The record's `ValueId` is MINTED BY THE
// CALLER and handed in, rather than returned beside the block — a pair would have to be a `type`, and that
// is a heap box (plus a destructor cascade over its `IrBlock` field) per call, for two values.
export function emitCStringToManaged(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cstr ValueId, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	// The LENGTH's id is minted here and handed down, exactly as `record`'s is and for the same reason: a
	// builder that returns both a block and a value would have to return a pair, and a pair is a heap box.
	let i = ids.mint()
	var build = emitCStringLength(module, func: func, cur: cur, cstr: cstr, length: i, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitManagedByteBuffer(module, block: build, record: record, byteCount: i, ids: ids)
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: build, dest: buffer, src: cstr, byteCount: i, ids: ids)
	// PUBLISH the bytes. `__managed_reserve` sets the capacity and leaves the length 0 (that is its contract, and
	// `__managed_mem_create`'s comment states it); `String.init` reads `length@8`, so an unpublished record would
	// answer with the empty string.
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, value: i)
	return afterCopy
end 'emitCStringToManaged'

// `__md_open_search(pathRec) -> (box, flag)` — open a Win32 search, or throw `__ManagedDirectoryError`.
//
// ⚠ **THE PENDING FLAG IS SET HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE.** `FindFirstFileA` has already written the first entry
// into the record by the time this returns; see `FindBlockPendingOffset` for what happens without it.
function buildManagedDirectoryOpenSearch(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedDirectoryThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdterm")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdfail")
	var opened = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let pathRec = 0 as ValueId

	let scratch = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterCopy, pathRec: pathRec, ids: ids)
	let blockSize = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: FindBlockBytes, ids: ids)
	let findBlock = emitRawAlloc(module, block: afterCopy, byteCount: blockSize, ids: ids)
	let findData = emitFindDataAddr(module, block: afterCopy, findBlock: findBlock, ids: ids)
	let search = ids.mint()
	emitFilesystemYield(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osFileFindFirst(search, pathPtrId: scratch, findDataPtrId: findData))
	// The last error is captured IMMEDIATELY after the call — a `__mm_free` in between runs Win32 code of its
	// own and could overwrite the thread's last-error. The same ordering `ManagedFileRuntime` states once.
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: InvalidHandleValue, ids: ids)
	let isInvalid = emitCmp(module, block: afterCopy, a: search, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(isInvalid, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: opened.id))

	// The find block is released on the failure path: the throw carries no box, so nothing else ever will.
	emitRawFree(module, block: failed, ptr: findBlock, ids: ids)
	emitDirectoryErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: failed, err: err, fallbackFlag: ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: opened, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockHandleOffset, value: search)
	let pending = emitConst(module, block: opened, value: FindEntryPending, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: opened, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockPendingOffset, value: pending)

	// The box: one 8-byte slot holding the find block's address, allocated through `__mm_alloc` so the
	// ordinary ownership machinery drops it — and the drop it takes is `__md_destruct`, which closes the
	// search and frees BOTH allocations (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`).
	let boxSize = emitConst(module, block: opened, value: ManagedDirectoryBoxBytes, ids: ids)
	let box = emitRawAlloc(module, block: opened, byteCount: boxSize, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: opened, record: box, offset: ManagedDirectoryBlockOffset, value: findBlock)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: opened, value: box, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryOpenSearch'

// ⭐⭐ **`__md_next(box) -> (found, flag)` — ADVANCE-FIRST, DOT-FILTERING, AND EOF AS A PLAIN 0.**
//
// The shape both references share: consume the pending entry if there is one, otherwise fetch; then test the
// entry against `.`/`..` and go round again if it is one; a real entry answers 1.
//
//   entry     → the closed guard (`ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow`)
//   checkPend → pending? consume it in place : fetch
//   fetch     → `FindNextFileA`; FALSE + `ERROR_NO_MORE_FILES` ⇒ EOF, FALSE + anything else ⇒ throw
//   dotCheck  → is the current name `.` or `..`? yes ⇒ back to `fetch`, no ⇒ found
//
// ⚠ **THE DOT TEST IS THREE BYTES, NOT ONE**, and each of the three matters: `.` is byte0 `.` + byte1 NUL,
// `..` is byte0 `.` + byte1 `.` + byte2 NUL, and a real `.gitignore` / `..config` must survive both. Testing
// only byte0 would hide every dotfile in the directory.
function buildManagedDirectoryNext(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryNextName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedDirectoryThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkPend = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdpend")
	var consume = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdconsume")
	var fetch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdfetch")
	var fetchFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdnofile")
	var atEof = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdeof")
	var fetchError = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdnexterr")
	var dotCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mddot")
	var dot1 = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mddot1")
	var dot2 = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mddot2")
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdfound")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let findBlock = emitLoadFindBlock(module, block: entry, box: box, ids: ids)

	// ⚠⚠ **THE `closed` GUARD IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM BOTH REFERENCES, AND IT PREVENTS A CRASH AS
	// WELL AS A WRONG ANSWER.** Neither reference ever throws `__ManagedDirectoryError.closed`; the
	// bootstrap's `next()` on a closed search reaches `FindNextFileA(NULL)` while the pending flag may still
	// claim an entry, so `d.close()` then `d.next()` either REPLAYS the first entry as found or faults.
	// MEASURED with the guard defeated: exit **0xC0000005**, an access violation.
	// `managed-directory.next-after-close-throws-closed` pins it. The guard's SHAPE is
	// `ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow`, shared with `__ManagedFile`'s `size`/`read`/`write`,
	// which throw `__ManagedFileError.closed` from the identical test for the identical reason — only the
	// record, the offset, the sentinel and the flag are this family's. The handle sits one level down (in
	// the find block, not in the box), which is exactly what the `record`/`handleOffset` pair carries. It is
	// called directly rather than through a directory-family wrapper because there is ONE site.
	let handle = emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: checkPend, record: findBlock, handleOffset: FindBlockHandleOffset, closedSentinel: ManagedDirectoryClosedHandle, closedFlag: ManagedDirectoryClosedFlag, ids: ids)

	// Pending? Then `FindFirstFileA`'s entry is still unconsumed: clear the flag and go straight to the dot
	// check, WITHOUT fetching. This is the half that keeps the first entry of every search.
	let pendingFlag = emitLoadField(module, block: checkPend, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockPendingOffset, ids: ids)
	let consumedConst = emitConst(module, block: checkPend, value: FindEntryConsumed, ids: ids)
	let alreadyConsumed = emitCmp(module, block: checkPend, a: pendingFlag, b: consumedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkPend, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyConsumed, thenBlockId: fetch.id, elseBlockId: consume.id))

	let clearPending = emitConst(module, block: consume, value: FindEntryConsumed, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: consume, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockPendingOffset, value: clearPending)
	module.setTerminator(consume, op: StdOp.branch(dotCheck.id))

	let findData = emitFindDataAddr(module, block: fetch, findBlock: findBlock, ids: ids)
	let verdict = ids.mint()
	emitFilesystemYield(module, block: fetch, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(fetch, op: StdOp.osFileFindNext(verdict, handleId: handle, findDataPtrId: findData))
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fetch, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	let falseConst = emitConst(module, block: fetch, value: Win32False, ids: ids)
	let noEntry = emitCmp(module, block: fetch, a: verdict, b: falseConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fetch, op: StdOp.condBranch(noEntry, thenBlockId: fetchFailed.id, elseBlockId: dotCheck.id))

	// ⭐ A FALSE means one of two very different things, and `GetLastError` is what tells them apart.
	let noMoreConst = emitConst(module, block: fetchFailed, value: ErrorNoMoreFiles, ids: ids)
	let isEof = emitCmp(module, block: fetchFailed, a: err, b: noMoreConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fetchFailed, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEof, thenBlockId: atEof.id, elseBlockId: fetchError.id))

	let complete = emitConst(module, block: atEof, value: DirectoryIterationComplete, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: atEof, value: complete, ids: ids)
	emitDirectoryErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: fetchError, err: err, fallbackFlag: ManagedDirectoryNextFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// `.`/`..` filtering. `name[0] != '.'` is a real entry at once; otherwise byte1 decides, and if byte1 is
	// also `.` then byte2 does.
	let name = emitFileNameAddr(module, block: dotCheck, findBlock: findBlock, ids: ids)
	let zeroIndex = emitConst(module, block: dotCheck, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let byte0 = emitLoadByte(module, block: dotCheck, base: name, index: zeroIndex, ids: ids)
	let dotConst = emitConst(module, block: dotCheck, value: DotByte, ids: ids)
	let leadingDot = emitCmp(module, block: dotCheck, a: byte0, b: dotConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dotCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(leadingDot, thenBlockId: dot1.id, elseBlockId: found.id))

	let oneIndex = emitConst(module, block: dot1, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let byte1 = emitLoadByte(module, block: dot1, base: name, index: oneIndex, ids: ids)
	let nul1 = emitConst(module, block: dot1, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	let isSingleDot = emitCmp(module, block: dot1, a: byte1, b: nul1, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	// A bare `.` is skipped; anything else with a leading dot needs byte2 to decide.
	module.setTerminator(dot1, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSingleDot, thenBlockId: fetch.id, elseBlockId: dot2.id))

	let secondDot = emitCmp(module, block: dot2, a: byte1, b: dotConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	var dot3 = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mddot3")
	module.setTerminator(dot2, op: StdOp.condBranch(secondDot, thenBlockId: dot3.id, elseBlockId: found.id))

	let twoIndex = emitConst(module, block: dot3, value: 2, ids: ids)
	let byte2 = emitLoadByte(module, block: dot3, base: name, index: twoIndex, ids: ids)
	let nul2 = emitConst(module, block: dot3, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	let isDoubleDot = emitCmp(module, block: dot3, a: byte2, b: nul2, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dot3, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDoubleDot, thenBlockId: fetch.id, elseBlockId: found.id))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: found, value: DirectoryEntryFound, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: found, value: one, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryNext'

// `__md_filename(box) -> arrRec` — the current entry's name as a FRESH owned `__ManagedMemory`. NON-throwing
// per the spec's "Instance Methods (non-throwing)": it reads a record the open already filled, so there is no
// call to fail.
//
// ⚠ **BEFORE THE FIRST `next()` AND AFTER EOF IT ANSWERS WITH WHATEVER THE RECORD HOLDS, AND THAT IS THE
// DOCUMENTED CONTRACT RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.** The spec says "Only valid after a `next()` call that
// returned non-zero"; Win32 does not clear `cFileName` when `FindNextFileA` reports end-of-iteration, so the
// last successful name stays there, and before the first `next()` the record holds `FindFirstFileA`'s entry.
// Both references answer identically. It is never a WILD read: `openSearch` only returns a box whose record
// the OS has filled, and neither `close()` nor the pending flag touches the name bytes.
function buildManagedDirectoryFilename(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryFilenameName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	let findBlock = emitLoadFindBlock(module, block: entry, box: box, ids: ids)
	let name = emitFileNameAddr(module, block: entry, findBlock: findBlock, ids: ids)
	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: entry, cstr: name, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryFilename'

// `__md_close(box)` — close the search handle, idempotently. Returns nothing and throws nothing, per the
// spec's "Idempotent. Also called automatically via destructor."
//
// ⭐ **IT DOES NOT FREE THE BLOCK — THE DESTRUCTOR DOES**, and v1's comment (`runtime.std:3303-3310`) names
// the bug that rule exists to stop: the block's single reference belongs to the OWNER (a `Directory.dir`
// field, a scope binding), whose own drop frees it, so a `close()` that released it too double-freed every
// explicitly-closed handle — the "`Directory.list` teardown trap". `search-and-list` runs exactly that
// sequence (an explicit `close()` and then a scope-exit drop of the wrapper).
//
// ⭐ **IDEMPOTENCE IS THE ZEROED HANDLE**, exactly as `__mf_close`'s is: the block is zeroed at allocation
// and this writes the closed sentinel back, so one guard covers "never opened" and "already closed".
// ⚠ It clears the PENDING flag as well, and that is not tidiness: the flag says "the record holds an entry
// nobody has consumed", which stops being true the moment the search is gone. Leaving it set would make a
// post-close `next()` replay the first entry — which the `closed` guard now refuses anyway, so this is the
// second of two independent reasons the replay cannot happen.
function buildManagedDirectoryClose(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryCloseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doClose = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mddo")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mddone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	let findBlock = emitLoadFindBlock(module, block: entry, box: box, ids: ids)
	let handle = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockHandleOffset, ids: ids)
	let closedConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ManagedDirectoryClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	let alreadyClosed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: handle, b: closedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyClosed, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: doClose.id))

	emitFilesystemYield(module, block: doClose, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doClose, op: StdOp.osFileFindClose(handle))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: doClose, value: ManagedDirectoryClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doClose, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockHandleOffset, value: zero)
	let consumed = emitConst(module, block: doClose, value: FindEntryConsumed, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doClose, record: findBlock, offset: FindBlockPendingOffset, value: consumed)
	module.setTerminator(doClose, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryClose'

// ⭐⭐ **`__md_destruct(box)` — THE RAII DESTRUCTOR, AND THE REASON A `__ManagedDirectory` MAY BE DROPPED AT
// ALL.** The refcount-checked teardown a box takes instead of the generic `__mm_decref`: last owner ⇒ close
// the search, free the find block, free the eight bytes; otherwise lower the count and keep the box.
//
// ⭐ It reuses `__md_close` rather than re-emitting the close, so idempotence is stated once: an explicit
// `d.close()` followed by the scope-exit drop finds the sentinel already stored and does nothing.
//
// ⚠ **IT FREES TWO ALLOCATIONS, WHICH IS THE WHOLE COST OF THE TWO-LEVEL RECORD**, and the ORDER matters:
// the find block is read (by `__md_close`) before it is freed, and the box is freed last because the block's
// address is read out of it.
//
// The skeleton is `MmRuntime.emitFieldCascadeDestructor`'s minus the field walk, and
// `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner` is shared with it — so "how many owners are left?" is asked one way by
// every destructor in the compiler.
function buildManagedDirectoryDestruct(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryDestructName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var drop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drop")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: box, lastOwner: drop, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(drop, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: ManagedDirectoryCloseName, args: argsOf(box)))
	let findBlock = emitLoadFindBlock(module, block: drop, box: box, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: drop, ptr: findBlock, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: drop, ptr: box, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(drop, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryDestruct'

// `__md_exists(pathRec) -> 0 | 1` — NON-throwing: "it is not a directory" is an answer, not a failure.
//
// ⭐ **THE ANSWER IS `bool` AT THE SURFACE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED FROM THE CORPUS RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** The
// two references disagree — the bootstrap registers `IrType.I64` (`2-Parser.cs:1458`), v1 `MaxonType.boolean`
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1915`, whose comment says bool is deliberate so `not` is logical negation and not
// bitwise complement) — and `specs/managed-directory.md:205` writes `if not __ManagedDirectory.exists(…)`,
// which settles it. v1 is right; the bootstrap is loose. The RUNTIME still answers a 0/1 i64 (this tier has
// no `setcc`); it is the PARSER that tags the result `ValueTypeTag.boolean`, which is exactly the arrangement
// `__managed_is_empty` already has.
//
// ⭐ **IT ASKS `GetFileAttributesA`, NOT `FindFirstFileA`**, which is the opposite of the choice
// `__mf_exists` makes — see the `osFileAttributes` op's comment for why the argument that forbids a second
// entry point there does not reach here: this takes a bare DIRECTORY path, and opening a search on one would
// cost a handle to answer a question about one bit.
function buildManagedDirectoryExists(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryExistsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdterm")
	var missing = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdmiss")
	var present = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdpresent")
	var isDir = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdisdir")
	var notDir = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdnotdir")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let pathRec = 0 as ValueId

	let scratch = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterCopy, pathRec: pathRec, ids: ids)
	let rawAttrs = ids.mint()
	emitFilesystemYield(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osFileAttributes(rawAttrs, pathPtrId: scratch))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	// `GetFileAttributesA` answers a DWORD, so the comparison is against the MASKED value: a raw i64 register
	// holding 0x00000000FFFFFFFF is not -1, and testing it against -1 would call every missing path present.
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: DwordMask, ids: ids)
	let attrs = emitBin(module, block: afterCopy, a: rawAttrs, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: InvalidFileAttributes, ids: ids)
	let noSuchPath = emitCmp(module, block: afterCopy, a: attrs, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(noSuchPath, thenBlockId: missing.id, elseBlockId: present.id))

	let zeroAnswer = emitConst(module, block: missing, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(missing, op: StdOp.ret(zeroAnswer))

	// The path exists — but `exists()` asks whether it is a DIRECTORY, so a plain file answers false.
	let dirMask = emitConst(module, block: present, value: FileAttributeDirectory, ids: ids)
	let dirBit = emitBin(module, block: present, a: attrs, b: dirMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let noBit = emitConst(module, block: present, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let directory = emitCmp(module, block: present, a: dirBit, b: noBit, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(present, op: StdOp.condBranch(directory, thenBlockId: isDir.id, elseBlockId: notDir.id))

	let yes = emitConst(module, block: isDir, value: 1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(isDir, op: StdOp.ret(yes))
	let no = emitConst(module, block: notDir, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notDir, op: StdOp.ret(no))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryExists'

// `__md_create(pathRec)` — VOID and throwing: `CreateDirectoryA(path, NULL)`, with a zero BOOL classified
// through the shared errno mapping (`createFailed` as the catch-all).
//
// ⚠ It is NOT the file family's `buildManagedFilePathOperation`, even though the skeleton rhymes: that
// builder is parameterized over `__ManagedFile`'s ops and its enum's flags, and threading a second enum
// through it would make one function speak for two error types. The shared parts — the scratch copy, the
// errno classification, the two exits — ARE shared; what is written here is the one call and its verdict.
function buildManagedDirectoryCreate(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryCreateName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedDirectoryThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdterm")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdfail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let pathRec = 0 as ValueId

	let scratch = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterCopy, pathRec: pathRec, ids: ids)
	let verdict = ids.mint()
	emitFilesystemYield(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osDirCreate(verdict, pathPtrId: scratch))
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	let falseConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: Win32False, ids: ids)
	let refused = emitCmp(module, block: afterCopy, a: verdict, b: falseConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(refused, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitDirectoryErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: failed, err: err, fallbackFlag: ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedFlag, ids: ids)
	emitVoidOkReturn(module, block: ok, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryCreate'

// ⭐⭐ **`__md_current_path() -> (arrRec, flag)` — `GetCurrentDirectoryA` WITH ITS GROW-AND-RETRY.**
//
// The API's answer means three different things (see `MaxPathBytes`), so the body is: try `MAX_PATH`; if it
// fits, copy out; if the OS names a larger size, free and try again at exactly that size; if either attempt
// answers 0, free and throw `currentPathFailed`.
//
// ⚠ **THE RETRY IS STRAIGHT-LINE RATHER THAN A LOOP, AND ITS THIRD EDGE IS A REFUSAL RATHER THAN DEAD
// CODE.** The second buffer is the size the OS itself named, so a second "too small" would be the OS
// contradicting its own answer — not something this code can rule out, and not something it may paper over
// either, because the buffer's contents in that case are UNDEFINED. So attempt 2's "did not fit" edge goes
// to the FAILURE exit: a thrown `currentPathFailed` rather than a copy out of an undefined buffer. The
// bootstrap loops instead (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:4088-4110`), which a `goto` makes free for it; here a
// loop would need the buffer AND its size carried through a merge for an iteration count that is one in
// every reachable case.
//
// ⚠ The raw cstring buffer is a scratch allocation this function owns end to end: it is freed before EVERY
// exit, the throwing ones included, and what the caller receives is the independent `__ManagedMemory` copy.
function buildManagedDirectoryCurrentPath(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedDirectoryThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var retry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdretry")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdfail")
	var copyOut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdcopy")

	// The buffer that reaches `retry`/`copyOut`/`failed` is one of two, so it rides a block-arg at each —
	// the phi model is the only way a value crosses a merge at this tier, and giving `retry` one as well is
	// what lets ONE verdict helper serve both attempts.
	let doomed = ids.mint()
	failed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(doomed, argType: StdType.i64))
	let buffer = ids.mint()
	copyOut.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(buffer, argType: StdType.i64))
	let outgrown = ids.mint()
	retry.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(outgrown, argType: StdType.i64))

	// Attempt 1, at MAX_PATH.
	let firstSize = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: MaxPathBytes, ids: ids)
	let firstBuf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: entry, byteCount: firstSize, ids: ids)
	let firstLen = ids.mint()
	emitFilesystemYield(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osDirCurrentPath(firstLen, bufLenId: firstSize, bufPtrId: firstBuf))
	// This attempt's buffer rides every exit as a block arg — the shared verdict classifies, the caller says
	// what crosses each merge (see `emitFilledBufferVerdict`'s seam paragraph).
	var firstFit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdcpfit")
	emitFilledBufferVerdict(module, block: entry, check: firstFit, written: firstLen, capacity: firstSize, whenRefused: failed, whenFits: copyOut, whenTooSmall: retry, ids: ids)
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(failed.id, argIds: argsOf(firstBuf)))
	firstFit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyOut.id, argIds: argsOf(firstBuf)))
	firstFit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(retry.id, argIds: argsOf(firstBuf)))

	// Attempt 2, at exactly the size the OS named (which INCLUDES the NUL, so it is one more than the path).
	// `firstLen` is defined in `entry`, which dominates this block.
	emitRawFree(module, block: retry, ptr: outgrown, ids: ids)
	let secondBuf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: retry, byteCount: firstLen, ids: ids)
	let secondLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(retry, op: StdOp.osDirCurrentPath(secondLen, bufLenId: firstLen, bufPtrId: secondBuf))
	// Attempt 2's "did not fit" edge goes to the FAILURE exit — see this builder's header for why a second
	// "too small" is the OS contradicting itself and must not be copied out of.
	var secondFit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mdcpfit")
	emitFilledBufferVerdict(module, block: retry, check: secondFit, written: secondLen, capacity: firstLen, whenRefused: failed, whenFits: copyOut, whenTooSmall: failed, ids: ids)
	retry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(failed.id, argIds: argsOf(secondBuf)))
	secondFit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyOut.id, argIds: argsOf(secondBuf)))
	secondFit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(failed.id, argIds: argsOf(secondBuf)))

	emitRawFree(module, block: failed, ptr: doomed, ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: failed, flag: ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: copyOut, cstr: buffer, record: record, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: built, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: built, value: record, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedDirectoryCurrentPath'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE ATTEMPT'S THREE-WAY VERDICT FOR A Win32 CALL THAT FILLS A CALLER'S BUFFER AND ANSWERS A COUNT.**
// `0` is a failure; `< capacity` means the answer is in the buffer, NUL included; anything else means the
// buffer was too small. Three answers, one classification, and the callers differ only in where each of
// the three goes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE `written == 0` TEST IS FIRST AND IT HAS TO BE: a 0 also satisfies `< capacity`**, so the fit test
// alone would route a failure into the copy and read a buffer the OS never wrote. That ordering is the ONE
// fact this function exists to hold — and it was written down TWICE, here and inline in `ProcessRuntime`'s
// `GetModuleFileNameA` loop, until the W5 review folded them together. Nothing made the two agree, and what
// a divergence produces is a read of undefined memory rather than a diagnostic.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN IS THE MERGE ARGUMENTS, AND THAT IS THE HONEST SEAM.** `__md_current_path`
// carries each attempt's buffer into all three exits as a block arg, because its two attempts reach ONE
// `copyOut`/`failed` pair from different blocks; `__proc_exe_path`'s exits are all dominated by its loop
// header, so its buffer is simply in scope and its edges carry nothing. The classification is one fact; the
// phi shape is two, so the caller pushes its own `branchEdges`.
//
// ⚠ The `check` block is the CALLER's to create, so each builder keeps its own block ORDER and its own
// label. It is the only block this verdict needs beyond the ones the caller already names.
export function emitFilledBufferVerdict(module StdModule, block IrBlock, check IrBlock, written ValueId, capacity ValueId, whenRefused IrBlock, whenFits IrBlock, whenTooSmall IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let refused = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: written, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(refused, thenBlockId: whenRefused.id, elseBlockId: check.id))

	let fits = emitCmp(module, block: check, a: written, b: capacity, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(check, op: StdOp.condBranch(fits, thenBlockId: whenFits.id, elseBlockId: whenTooSmall.id))
end 'emitFilledBufferVerdict'

// ⭐ **INSTALL + THE BIDIRECTIONAL DRIFT GUARD**, copied from `installManagedFileRuntime` for its stated
// reason: the predicate above and the `throws` clauses stamped by the builders are ONE fact in two places,
// and a builder that gains or loses a clause without the predicate following is a `try` that silently stops
// being required (or an E3055 on a call that cannot fail). Checked in BOTH directions.
// The name this family's ordinals are transcribed under, for `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s diagnostic — one
// constant so the lookup's message and the claim's message name the same author.
let ManagedDirectoryOrdinalInstaller = b"the __ManagedDirectory runtime"

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD FOR `__ManagedDirectoryError` (7b, re-anchored at A1s)** — the twin of
// `verifyManagedFileOrdinals`, which carries the whole argument: the case ORDER is the wire format, the
// declaration it is verified against is `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s, and it is a free function because it
// needs the `Project` and must run on every compile.
export function verifyManagedDirectoryOrdinals(project Project)
	match declaredBuiltinErrorEnum(project, typeName: ManagedDirectoryErrorTypeName, installer: ManagedDirectoryOrdinalInstaller) 'declaration'
		notDeclared then return
		found(declaration) then claimManagedDirectoryOrdinals(project, declaration: declaration)
	end 'declaration'
end 'verifyManagedDirectoryOrdinals'

function claimManagedDirectoryOrdinals(project Project, declaration EnumLayout)
	var directoryErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overDeclared(project, declaration: declaration, installer: ManagedDirectoryOrdinalInstaller)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryNotFoundCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryNotFoundOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryAccessDeniedOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryOpenSearchFailedOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryNextFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryNextFailedOrdinal)
	// `filename()` on an exhausted iterator is non-throwing here and in both references — see the ordinal
	// block at the top of this file.
	directoryErrors.neverThrown(ManagedDirectoryIteratorInvalidCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryIteratorInvalidOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryCreateFailedOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryCurrentPathFailedOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.claim(ManagedDirectoryClosedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedDirectoryClosedOrdinal)
	directoryErrors.requireTotal()
end 'claimManagedDirectoryOrdinals'

export function installManagedDirectoryRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if not usage.usesManagedDirectory 'noManagedDirectory'
		return
	end 'noManagedDirectory'

	buildManagedDirectoryOpenSearch(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryNext(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryFilename(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryClose(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryDestruct(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryExists(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryCreate(module)
	buildManagedDirectoryCurrentPath(module)

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(func.name) 'notOurs'
			continue
		end 'notOurs'
		let named = isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(func.name)
		let stamped = throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName)
		if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
			panic("installManagedDirectoryRuntime: '{String.from(func.name)}' stamps a `throws` clause but isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee does NOT name it — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted (R4.3)")
		end 'stampedNotNamed'
		if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
			panic("installManagedDirectoryRuntime: isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee names '{String.from(func.name)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted (R4.3)")
		end 'namedNotStamped'

		// ⭐ **THE THIRD LIST (D12 review): a throwing callee must also have a SOURCE-METHOD arm.** R4.3's map
		// is the OLDEST instance of this exposure — it shipped guarded only by its own panic — and D12 turned
		// one instance into four. Asked, not described: the CALL is the check, so a missing arm fires here at
		// install rather than in front of a user who forgot a `try`. Reasoning lives once, on
		// `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`.
		if named 'hasSourceMethod'
			requireQuotableSourceSpelling(managedDirectorySourceMethodName(func.name), callee: func.name)
		end 'hasSourceMethod'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'installManagedDirectoryRuntime'

// Every callee this file installs — what the drift guard iterates, what `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set
// `usesManagedDirectory`, and what `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses on a non-Windows
// target. A PREFIX test rather than an eight-way `or`: every entry point this file installs is `__md_`-named
// by construction, so a ninth is covered without being remembered here as well as declared above.
export function isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ManagedDirectoryRuntimePrefix)
end 'isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee'
